    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d499">

                <pb facs="0719" n="703" sameAs="frus:frus1948v09/pg703"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">834.00/8–948: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Embassy in Brazil
                        </hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d499-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1948-08-09T19:00:00-04:00">August 9, 1948—7
                            p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>596. Brazilian Emb has informed Dept of rumors referred to in Asunción tel 369,
                    Aug. 7, 7 PM<anchor n="2" xml:id="d499-f2"/> re alleged Argentine aggressive
                    intentions against Paraguay and in support of Paraguayan revolutionaries.
                    Brazilian Emb here was informed that Dept had no info of such an alarmist
                    character and could not confirm these reports. There was some discussion as to
                    whether this situation might properly be put before the “Committee of Five”
                    established by Resolution 14 of Habana Consultative Meeting of 1940<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d499-f3"/> (which recently held its first meeting in Washington) but
                    no suggestions were made in this regard. Brazilians thought we might wish to
                    inform Argentine Govt of our concern at these rumors. They were told the Dept
                    would discuss the matter with Amb Bruce who is now in Washington. Dept expressed
                    hope that all countries concerned would be bound by inter-American
                    non-intervention commitments.</p>
                <p>Telegraph substance any further discussions you may have with FonMin or high govt
                    officials on this subject.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Marshall</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d499-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d499-f1-content">
                <p>Repeated to Buenos Aires as 723, to Montevideo as 161, and to Asuncion as
                    138.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d499-f2" n="2" xml:id="d499-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d499-f3" n="3" xml:id="d499-f3-content">
                <p>See resolution XIV, Final Act and Convention of the Habana meeting of the
                    Ministers of Foreign Affairs, in the Department of State <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bulletin</hi>, August 24, 1940, p. 136.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
